<p><img decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1067799" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181603/NASAartWArhol.jpg" alt width="800" height="600" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181603/NASAartWArhol.jpg 800w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181603/NASAartWArhol-240x180.jpg 240w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181603/NASAartWArhol-360x270.jpg 360w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181603/NASAartWArhol-768x576.jpg 768w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181603/NASAartWArhol-300x225.jpg 300w" sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px"></p>
<p>It’s hard to imagine that the&nbsp;<a href="https://rocketkids.omeka.net/space-craze-cultural-context">space-crazed general public</a> needed any help getting worked up about astronauts and NASA in the early ’60s.</p>
<p>Perhaps the wild popularity of space-related imagery was in part what motivated NASA administrator <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_E._Webb">James Webb</a> to create the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.nasa.gov/collaborating-with-nasa/nasa-art-program/">NASA Art Program</a>&nbsp;in 1962.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignleft size-full wp-image-1067812" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08203659/normanrockwellnasa.jpg" alt width="1024" height="676" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08203659/normanrockwellnasa.jpg 1024w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08203659/normanrockwellnasa-240x158.jpg 240w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08203659/normanrockwellnasa-360x238.jpg 360w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08203659/normanrockwellnasa-768x507.jpg 768w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08203659/normanrockwellnasa-300x198.jpg 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px"></p>
<p>Although the program’s handpicked artists weren’t edited or censored in any way, they were briefed on how NASA hoped to be represented, and which emotions their creations were meant to capture—the excitement and uncertainty of exploring these frontiers.</p>
<p>NASA was also careful to collect everything the artists produced while participating in the program, from sketches to finished work.</p>
<div class="oc-center-da">
<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p>In turn, the artists received unprecedented access to launch sites, key personnel, and major events such as <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/project-mercury/">Project Mercury</a> and the <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/mission/apollo-11/">Apollo 11 Mission</a>.</p>
<p>Hundreds of artists, including Andy Warhol, <a href="https://gizmodo.com/nasa-loaned-norman-rockwell-a-spacesuit-for-this-pictur-1224192234">Norman Rockwell</a>, and <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2018/10/laurie-andersons-new-virtual-reality-installation-takes-viewers-unconventional-tour-moon.html">Laurie Anderson</a>, have brought their unique sensibilities to the project. (Find NASA-inspired art by Warhol and Rockwell above.)</p>
<p>(And hey, no shame if you mistakenly assumed <a href="https://canvas.saatchiart.com/art/art-history-101/out-of-this-world-andy-warhols-moonwalk">Warhol’s 1987 <em>Moonwalk 1</em> was created as a promo for MTV</a>…)</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone wp-image-1067800 size-full" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181623/NASAartWythe-e1565321471702.jpg" alt width="900" height="610"></p>
<p><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jamie_Wyeth">Jamie Wyeth</a>’s 1964 watercolor<em> Gemini Launch Pad</em> includes a humble bicycle, the means by which technicians traveled back and forth between the launch pad and the reinforced-concrete blockhouse where they worked.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone wp-image-1067801 size-full" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181643/NASAartLeibovitz-e1565321487776.jpg" alt width="900" height="1125"></p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1067802" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181701/NASAartLeibovitz2.jpg" alt width="857" height="1200" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181701/NASAartLeibovitz2.jpg 857w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181701/NASAartLeibovitz2-171x240.jpg 171w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181701/NASAartLeibovitz2-257x360.jpg 257w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181701/NASAartLeibovitz2-768x1075.jpg 768w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181701/NASAartLeibovitz2-731x1024.jpg 731w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181701/NASAartLeibovitz2-300x420.jpg 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 857px) 100vw, 857px"></p>
<p><a href="https://www.theartstory.org/artist/leibovitz-annie/">Photographer Annie Leibovitz</a> offers two views of NASA’s first female pilot and commander, <a href="https://www.womenofthehall.org/inductee/eileen-collins/">Eileen Collins</a>—with and without helmet.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1067803" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181722/NASAartStamp.jpg" alt width="650" height="615" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181722/NASAartStamp.jpg 650w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181722/NASAartStamp-240x227.jpg 240w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181722/NASAartStamp-360x341.jpg 360w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181722/NASAartStamp-300x284.jpg 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 650px) 100vw, 650px"></p>
<p>Postage stamp designer,&nbsp;<a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2010/12/31/arts/design/31calle.html">Paul Calle</a>, one of the inaugural group of participating artists, produced a stamp commemorating the Gemini 4 space capsule in celebration of NASA’s 9th anniversary. When <a href="https://www.callespaceart.com/Apollo_XI_Suiting_Up_Sketches.html">the Apollo 11 astronauts suited up</a> prior to blastoff on July 16, 1969, Calle was the only artist present. His quickly rendered felt-tip marker sketches lend a backstage element to the heroic iconography surrounding astronauts Armstrong, Aldrin and Collins. One of the items they carried with them on their journey was the engraved printing plate of <a href="https://www.callespaceart.com/Philatelic_-_Postage_stamp_Designs.html#10">Calle’s 1967 commemorative stamp</a>. They hand-canceled a proof aboard the flight, on the assumption that post offices might be hard to come by on the moon.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone wp-image-1067804 size-full" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2019/08/08181748/NASAartMars-e1565321519796.jpg" alt width="900" height="1350"></p>
<p><a href="https://www.openculture.com/2016/02/14-free-posters-from-nasa-that-depict-the-future-of-space-travel.html">In 2016, NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory enlisted a team of nine artists, designers, and illustrators</a> to collaborate on 14 posters, a visual throwback to the ones the WPA created between 1938 and 1941 to spark public interest in the National Parks. You can see the results at the&nbsp;<a href="https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/immersive/exoplanet-travel-bureau/">Exoplanet Travel Bureau</a>.</p>
<p>More recently, in 2024, NASA and the artist team Geraluz and WERC relaunched the program with <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/collaborating-with-nasa/nasa-art-program/">space-themed murals in Manhattan</a>.</p>
